generic_control_command_failed.description:
RainPoint rejected a control command sent through generic control.

- **Model:** {model}
- **Error:** {error}

Generic control uses an unverified mapping from RainPoint's product catalog rather than a tested per-model decoder, so this device's behavior is not fully proven.

As far as this integration can tell, the device's state was not changed by the failed command.

If this keeps happening, you can turn generic control off in the integration's options.

device_not_reporting.description:
RainPoint lists this device on your account, but the cloud has returned no readings for it across the last {missed_polls} checks, which run a couple of minutes apart.

- **Model:** {model}
- **Address:** {address}
- **Hub:** {hub_name}

This can happen when a device is paired over Bluetooth only, is out of range, or is switched off. The integration keeps checking and will clear this notice on its own once readings resume.

If this is a Bluetooth device, check whether it can also pair to a RainPoint hub. A Bluetooth-only device reports nothing to the cloud, so there is nothing for this integration to read; paired to a hub, the same device usually reports its readings and you will see far more of it here. Opening and closing it from Home Assistant may still be unavailable depending on the model, though that may come in a future release.

A **Not Reporting** entity was added for this device at the same time as this notice. You will find it on the device's own page under Diagnostic, and its attributes carry a pre-filled report link if you believe the device should be reporting.

orphaned_device_entities.description:
RainPoint has stopped listing this device on its hub, and has not listed it across the last {missed_polls} checks, which run a couple of minutes apart.

- **Device:** {sub_name}
- **Model:** {model}
- **Address:** {address}
- **Hub:** {hub_name}

Up to {entity_count} entities this integration created for it in this session are now permanently unavailable.

This usually means RainPoint moved the device to a different parent record on its side. When that happens a fresh set of entities for the same hardware already exists alongside these leftover ones, so you may be seeing each reading twice.

Removing the leftover entities also removes the history recorded against them, and that cannot be undone.
